+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> Use a <strong>list</strong> when you need to access elements multiple times, index them, or know the length. Use a <strong>generator</strong> when you are processing data once in a pipeline, dealing with large/infinite sequences, or chaining transformations. In ML, generators power data loaders (PyTorch <code>DataLoader</code>), streaming tokenization, and batch processing.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Regular Expressions</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Regular expressions (regex)</strong> are a mini-language for describing text patterns. In NLP, they are indispensable: cleaning raw text, extracting entities, tokenizing sentences, validating formats, and building rule-based systems. Python\'s <code>re</code> module provides a powerful regex engine that every NLP engineer must master.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Raw strings</strong> — Always use raw strings (<code>r"..."</code>) for regex patterns. Without them, Python interprets backslashes before the regex engine sees them: <code>"\\d"</code> becomes <code>\d</code>, but <code>r"\\d"</code> stays as <code>\\d</code>. Raw strings prevent this double-escaping headache.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Metacharacter Reference Card</strong> — These are the building blocks of every regex pattern:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">.</div><div class="cc-title">. (dot)</div><div class="cc-body">Any character except newline. <code>r"h.t"</code> matches "hat", "hit", "hot".</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">^$</div><div class="cc-title">^ and $</div><div class="cc-body">Start/end of string (or line with MULTILINE). <code>r"^Hello"</code> matches lines starting with "Hello".</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">*+?</div><div class="cc-title">* + ?</div><div class="cc-body">0+, 1+, 0 or 1 repetitions. <code>r"ab*c"</code> matches "ac", "abc", "abbc". Add <code>?</code> for non-greedy.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">{}</div><div class="cc-title">{n,m}</div><div class="cc-body">Exactly n, or n to m repetitions. <code>r"\\d{3,4}"</code> matches 3-4 digit numbers.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">[]</div><div class="cc-title">[abc]</div><div class="cc-body">Character class — matches any one. <code>[a-zA-Z]</code> = any letter. <code>[^0-9]</code> = NOT a digit.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">|</div><div class="cc-title">a|b</div><div class="cc-body">Alternation — matches a OR b. <code>r"cat|dog"</code> matches either word.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">\\</div><div class="cc-title">\\d \\w \\s</div><div class="cc-body"><code>\\d</code> = digit, <code>\\w</code> = word char, <code>\\s</code> = whitespace. Uppercase = negated: <code>\\D</code> = non-digit.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">()</div><div class="cc-title">Groups</div><div class="cc-body">Capture groups with <code>()</code>. Named: <code>(?P&lt;name&gt;...)</code>. Non-capturing: <code>(?:...)</code>.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Core <code>re</code> functions</strong> — The module provides several functions for different use cases:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> re\n\ntext = <span class="str">"Call us at 555-1234 or 555-5678. Email: info@example.com"</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># search() — first match anywhere in string</span>\nm = re.search(<span class="str">r"\\d{3}-\\d{4}"</span>, text)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(m.group())   <span class="cm"># "555-1234"</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(m.span())    <span class="cm"># (11, 19) — start, end indices</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># findall() — all non-overlapping matches</span>\nphones = re.findall(<span class="str">r"\\d{3}-\\d{4}"</span>, text)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(phones)  <span class="cm"># ["555-1234", "555-5678"]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># match() — only matches at the BEGINNING of string</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(re.match(<span class="str">r"Call"</span>, text))    <span class="cm"># Match object</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(re.match(<span class="str">r"Email"</span>, text))   <span class="cm"># None (not at start)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># sub() — find and replace with regex</span>\ncleaned = re.sub(<span class="str">r"\\d{3}-\\d{4}"</span>, <span class="str">"[REDACTED]"</span>, text)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(cleaned)  <span class="cm"># "Call us at [REDACTED] or [REDACTED]..."</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># split() — split by pattern</span>\ntokens = re.split(<span class="str">r"[\\s.,;:!?]+"</span>, <span class="str">"Hello, world! How are you?"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(tokens)  <span class="cm"># ["Hello", "world", "How", "are", "you", ""]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># compile() — pre-compile for reuse (faster in loops)</span>\nemail_pat = re.compile(<span class="str">r"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(email_pat.findall(text))  <span class="cm"># ["info@example.com"]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Groups and Named Groups</strong> — Groups capture subpatterns within matches. Named groups make complex patterns readable, which is critical for maintainable NLP code:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> re\n\n<span class="cm"># Named groups for structured extraction</span>\ndate_pat = re.compile(<span class="str">r"(?P&lt;year&gt;\\d{4})-(?P&lt;month&gt;\\d{2})-(?P&lt;day&gt;\\d{2})"</span>)\nm = date_pat.search(<span class="str">"Event on 2024-03-15"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(m.group(<span class="str">"year"</span>))   <span class="cm"># "2024"</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(m.group(<span class="str">"month"</span>))  <span class="cm"># "03"</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(m.groupdict())    <span class="cm"># {"year": "2024", "month": "03", "day": "15"}</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Lookahead/lookbehind — match without consuming</span>\n<span class="cm"># (?=...) positive lookahead  |  (?!...) negative lookahead</span>\n<span class="cm"># (?<=...) positive lookbehind |  (?<!...) negative lookbehind</span>\nprices = <span class="str">"$10 $20 €30 $40"</span>\nusd = re.findall(<span class="str">r"(?<=\\$)\\d+"</span>, prices)  <span class="cm"># ["10", "20", "40"]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># NLP Example: Clean text for tokenization</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">clean_text</span>(text):\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"http\\S+"</span>, <span class="str">""</span>, text)           <span class="cm"># remove URLs</span>\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"@\\w+"</span>, <span class="str">""</span>, text)              <span class="cm"># remove @mentions</span>\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"#(\\w+)"</span>, <span class="str">r"\\1"</span>, text)        <span class="cm"># hashtag → word</span>\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"[^a-zA-Z\\s]"</span>, <span class="str">""</span>, text)       <span class="cm"># keep only letters</span>\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"\\s+"</span>, <span class="str">" "</span>, text).strip()     <span class="cm"># normalize whitespace</span>\n    <span class="kw">return</span> text.lower()\n\ntweet = <span class="str">"@NLPexpert Great talk! #DeepLearning http://link.co 🔥🔥"</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">clean_text</span>(tweet))  <span class="cm"># "great talk deeplearning"</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Why do NLP engineers still use regex when we have transformers? Because regex handles the <em>preprocessing</em> stage: stripping HTML tags, normalizing Unicode, splitting sentences, extracting structured fields from messy text. No matter how sophisticated your model, garbage in means garbage out. Regex is the first line of defense in every text pipeline.</div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Performance tip:</strong> Always use <code>re.compile()</code> when applying the same pattern to many strings (e.g., cleaning a corpus). The compiled pattern avoids re-parsing the regex on each call. For simple string operations (starts with, contains, ends with), use built-in string methods — they are faster than regex.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: FUNCTIONAL PROGRAMMING
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Functional Programming</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Functional programming (FP)</strong> treats computation as the evaluation of <em>pure functions</em> — functions with no side effects whose output depends only on their input. Python is not a purely functional language, but it borrows powerful FP tools: <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>reduce</code>, <code>lambda</code>, closures, and the <code>functools</code>/<code>itertools</code> modules. These tools enable concise, composable, and parallelizable data transformations.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Pure functions and higher-order functions</strong> — A pure function always returns the same output for the same input and modifies nothing outside itself. A higher-order function takes functions as arguments or returns functions:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-left"><div class="compare-title">Impure (side effects)</div><div class="compare-body"><pre>total = 0\ndef add_to_total(x):\n    global total\n    total += x  # modifies global!\n    return total</pre></div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-right"><div class="compare-title">Pure (no side effects)</div><div class="compare-body"><pre>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b  # same input\n    # always → same output\n    # no external mutation</pre></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># map, filter, reduce — the FP trinity</span>\nnumbers = [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>, <span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">9</span>, <span class="num">10</span>]\n\n<span class="cm"># map: apply function to each element</span>\nsquared = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">map</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x**<span class="num">2</span>, numbers))\n\n<span class="cm"># filter: keep elements where function returns True</span>\nevens = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">filter</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>, numbers))\n\n<span class="cm"># reduce: accumulate values into single result</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> functools <span class="kw">import</span> reduce\nproduct = <span class="fn">reduce</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> acc, x: acc * x, numbers)  <span class="cm"># 3628800</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Pythonic equivalent — list comprehensions are often preferred</span>\nsquared = [x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> numbers]\nevens = [x <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> numbers <span class="kw">if</span> x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>]\n\n<span class="cm"># Higher-order function — returns a function</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">make_multiplier</span>(factor):\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">multiplier</span>(x):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> x * factor\n    <span class="kw">return</span> multiplier\n\ndouble = <span class="fn">make_multiplier</span>(<span class="num">2</span>)\ntriple = <span class="fn">make_multiplier</span>(<span class="num">3</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">double</span>(<span class="num">5</span>))  <span class="cm"># 10</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">triple</span>(<span class="num">5</span>))  <span class="cm"># 15</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>functools — essential tools</strong> for functional Python. These are not academic curiosities — they solve real problems in production ML code:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1f680;</div><div class="cc-title">lru_cache</div><div class="cc-body">Memoize function results. Ideal for expensive computations with repeated inputs. <code>@lru_cache(maxsize=128)</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1f4cc;</div><div class="cc-title">partial</div><div class="cc-body">Freeze some arguments of a function. <code>partial(pow, 2)</code> creates a "power of 2" function. Great for callbacks.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1f3ad;</div><div class="cc-title">wraps</div><div class="cc-body">Preserves original function metadata when writing decorators. Always use <code>@wraps(func)</code> in custom decorators.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x2211;</div><div class="cc-title">reduce</div><div class="cc-body">Fold a sequence into a single value using a binary function. <code>reduce(add, [1,2,3])</code> = 6.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">from</span> functools <span class="kw">import</span> lru_cache, partial, wraps\n<span class="kw">import</span> time\n\n<span class="cm"># lru_cache — memoization for expensive functions</span>\n<span class="kw">@lru_cache</span>(maxsize=<span class="num">256</span>)\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">expensive_nlp</span>(text):\n    <span class="str">"""Simulate expensive NLP processing."""</span>\n    time.sleep(<span class="num">1</span>)  <span class="cm"># pretend this takes 1 second</span>\n    <span class="kw">return</span> text.upper()\n\n<span class="fn">expensive_nlp</span>(<span class="str">"hello"</span>)  <span class="cm"># takes 1 second</span>\n<span class="fn">expensive_nlp</span>(<span class="str">"hello"</span>)  <span class="cm"># instant — cached!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># partial — freeze arguments for callbacks</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train</span>(model, lr, epochs, data):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Training {model} | lr={lr} | epochs={epochs}"</span>)\n\ntrain_bert = <span class="fn">partial</span>(train, <span class="str">"bert-base"</span>, <span class="num">2e-5</span>)\n<span class="fn">train_bert</span>(epochs=<span class="num">3</span>, data=<span class="str">"dataset"</span>)  <span class="cm"># model and lr are fixed</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># wraps — proper decorator pattern</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">timer</span>(func):\n    <span class="kw">@wraps</span>(func)  <span class="cm"># preserves func.__name__, __doc__</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">wrapper</span>(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        result = <span class="fn">func</span>(*args, **kwargs)\n        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{func.__name__} took {elapsed:.4f}s"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> result\n    <span class="kw">return</span> wrapper\n\n<span class="kw">@timer</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_data</span>(n):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">sum</span>(i**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(n))\n\n<span class="fn">process_data</span>(<span class="num">1_000_000</span>)  <span class="cm"># "process_data took 0.0823s"</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>itertools — the infinite toolbox</strong> for iterator algebra. These composable building blocks create efficient pipelines for data processing:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">from</span> itertools <span class="kw">import</span> (\n    islice, chain, takewhile, dropwhile,\n    starmap, tee, groupby, product, combinations\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># islice — slice any iterator (works with generators!)</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> itertools <span class="kw">import</span> count\nfirst_5_evens = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">islice</span>((x <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">count</span>() <span class="kw">if</span> x%<span class="num">2</span>==<span class="num">0</span>), <span class="num">5</span>))\n<span class="cm"># [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># chain — concatenate iterables lazily</span>\nall_data = <span class="fn">chain</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">3</span>), <span class="str">"abc"</span>, [<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>])\n<span class="cm"># 0, 1, 2, "a", "b", "c", 10, 20</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># takewhile / dropwhile — conditional slicing</span>\nnums = [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">6</span>]\nascending = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">takewhile</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x < <span class="num">7</span>, nums))  <span class="cm"># [1, 3, 5]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># tee — duplicate an iterator (both can be consumed independently)</span>\norig, backup = <span class="fn">tee</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">5</span>))\n\n<span class="cm"># product — Cartesian product (great for hyperparameter grids!)</span>\nlrs = [<span class="num">1e-3</span>, <span class="num">1e-4</span>, <span class="num">1e-5</span>]\nbatch_sizes = [<span class="num">16</span>, <span class="num">32</span>, <span class="num">64</span>]\n<span class="kw">for</span> lr, bs <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">product</span>(lrs, batch_sizes):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"lr={lr}, batch={bs}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># 9 combinations</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Style note:</strong> Python is a multi-paradigm language. The Pythonic approach often favors list comprehensions over <code>map</code>/<code>filter</code>, and explicit loops over complex <code>reduce</code> chains. Use FP tools when they make code clearer — not just because they exist. <code>lru_cache</code>, <code>partial</code>, and <code>itertools</code> are the FP tools you will reach for most often in practice.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: TYPE HINTS & STATIC ANALYSIS
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Type Hints & Static Analysis</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Python is dynamically typed, but that does not mean untyped.</strong> Type hints (PEP 484) let you annotate function signatures and variables with expected types. They are not enforced at runtime — Python ignores them during execution. Instead, tools like <strong>mypy</strong> check them <em>statically</em> (before running), catching bugs that would otherwise surface only in production. For ML codebases with complex data transformations, type hints are a lifesaver.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Basic type annotations</strong> — The syntax is clean: use <code>:</code> after parameters and <code>-></code> for return types:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Basic annotations</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">greet</span>(name: <span class="fn">str</span>, times: <span class="fn">int</span> = <span class="num">1</span>) -> <span class="fn">str</span>:\n    <span class="kw">return</span> (name + <span class="str">"! "</span>) * times\n\n<span class="cm"># Variable annotations</span>\ncount: <span class="fn">int</span> = <span class="num">0</span>\nnames: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>] = [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>]\nscores: <span class="fn">dict</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>, <span class="fn">float</span>] = {<span class="str">"Alice"</span>: <span class="num">0.95</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>: <span class="num">0.87</span>}\n\n<span class="cm"># Python 3.10+ union syntax (old: Union[int, str])</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process</span>(value: <span class="fn">int</span> | <span class="fn">str</span>) -> <span class="fn">str</span>:\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">str</span>(value)\n\n<span class="cm"># Optional = Union[X, None] → X | None in 3.10+</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">find_user</span>(user_id: <span class="fn">int</span>) -> <span class="fn">str</span> | <span class="kw">None</span>:\n    db = {<span class="num">1</span>: <span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="num">2</span>: <span class="str">"Bob"</span>}\n    <span class="kw">return</span> db.get(user_id)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Advanced types</strong> — For complex ML pipelines, you need more sophisticated type constructs:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">from</span> typing <span class="kw">import</span> TypeVar, Generic, Protocol, Callable, Iterator\n<span class="kw">from</span> collections.abc <span class="kw">import</span> Sequence\n\n<span class="cm"># TypeVar — generic functions</span>\nT = TypeVar(<span class="str">"T"</span>)\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">first</span>(items: Sequence[T]) -> T:\n    <span class="str">"""Return first element, preserving type info."""</span>\n    <span class="kw">return</span> items[<span class="num">0</span>]\n\nname = <span class="fn">first</span>([<span class="str">"a"</span>, <span class="str">"b"</span>])    <span class="cm"># mypy knows: str</span>\nnum = <span class="fn">first</span>([<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>])      <span class="cm"># mypy knows: int</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Protocol — structural subtyping ("duck typing with types")</span>\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Tokenizer</span>(Protocol):\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">tokenize</span>(<span class="kw">self</span>, text: <span class="fn">str</span>) -> <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>]: ...\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">vocab_size</span>(<span class="kw">self</span>) -> <span class="fn">int</span>: ...\n\n<span class="cm"># Any class with tokenize() and vocab_size() satisfies this</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">preprocess</span>(tok: Tokenizer, corpus: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>]) -> <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>]]:\n    <span class="kw">return</span> [tok.tokenize(doc) <span class="kw">for</span> doc <span class="kw">in</span> corpus]\n\n<span class="cm"># Callable — type for functions passed as arguments</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">apply_transform</span>(\n    data: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>],\n    transform: Callable[[<span class="fn">str</span>], <span class="fn">str</span>]\n) -> <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>]:\n    <span class="kw">return</span> [<span class="fn">transform</span>(item) <span class="kw">for</span> item <span class="kw">in</span> data]\n\n<span class="cm"># TypeAlias — readable complex types</span>\ntype Embedding = <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">float</span>]\ntype EmbeddingMatrix = <span class="fn">list</span>[Embedding]\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">cosine_sim</span>(a: Embedding, b: Embedding) -> <span class="fn">float</span>:\n    dot = <span class="fn">sum</span>(x*y <span class="kw">for</span> x, y <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">zip</span>(a, b))\n    norm_a = <span class="fn">sum</span>(x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> a) ** <span class="num">0.5</span>\n    norm_b = <span class="fn">sum</span>(x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> b) ** <span class="num">0.5</span>\n    <span class="kw">return</span> dot / (norm_a * norm_b)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Add type hints</strong> to your function signatures. Start with public APIs and function boundaries — these provide the most value per annotation.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Install mypy:</strong> <code>pip install mypy</code>. Run with <code>mypy your_module.py</code>. It will catch type mismatches, missing returns, invalid attribute access, and more.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Configure mypy</strong> in <code>pyproject.toml</code>: start with <code>--strict</code> mode on new projects, or <code>--warn-return-any</code> on existing ones. Gradually increase strictness.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Use in CI/CD:</strong> Add mypy to your GitHub Actions or pre-commit hooks. Catch type errors before they reach production. This is standard practice in professional ML teams.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Why do ML teams adopt type hints? Because ML code has complex data flows: a function might accept a DataFrame, a numpy array, or a torch Tensor. Without types, passing the wrong structure causes silent bugs — the model trains on garbage data and you only discover it after hours of GPU time. Type hints make these contracts explicit and machine-checkable.</div>'

