/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: CONCURRENCY BASICS
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Concurrency Basics</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Concurrency is doing multiple things at once.</strong> Python offers three approaches: <strong>threading</strong> (concurrent I/O), <strong>multiprocessing</strong> (parallel CPU work), and <strong>asyncio</strong> (cooperative multitasking). The right choice depends on whether your workload is <em>I/O-bound</em> (waiting for networks, files, APIs) or <em>CPU-bound</em> (computing, training, matrix math). Choosing wrong can make your code <em>slower</em> than sequential.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>The GIL — Python\'s infamous limitation:</strong> The Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) ensures only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time. This means threading does NOT speed up CPU-bound work — threads just take turns holding the GIL. However, the GIL is released during I/O operations (network, file, sleep), so threading works perfectly for I/O-bound tasks.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-left"><div class="compare-title">I/O-Bound (use threading/asyncio)</div><div class="compare-body">Downloading files, API calls, database queries, reading files. CPU is idle most of the time — it is waiting for external data. Threads release the GIL during I/O.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-right"><div class="compare-title">CPU-Bound (use multiprocessing)</div><div class="compare-body">Number crunching, image processing, model training (pure Python parts). CPU is maxed out. Need separate processes to bypass the GIL and use multiple cores.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> time\n<span class="kw">from</span> concurrent.futures <span class="kw">import</span> ThreadPoolExecutor, ProcessPoolExecutor\n\n<span class="cm"># ── I/O-Bound Example: Downloading URLs ──</span>\n<span class="kw">import</span> urllib.request\n\nurls = [\n    <span class="str">"https://example.com/page1"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"https://example.com/page2"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"https://example.com/page3"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"https://example.com/page4"</span>,\n]\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">fetch</span>(url):\n    <span class="kw">with</span> urllib.request.urlopen(url) <span class="kw">as</span> resp:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(resp.read())\n\n<span class="cm"># Sequential: ~4 seconds (1s per request)</span>\nstart = time.time()\nresults = [<span class="fn">fetch</span>(u) <span class="kw">for</span> u <span class="kw">in</span> urls]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Sequential: {time.time()-start:.1f}s"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Threaded: ~1 second (all requests overlap)</span>\nstart = time.time()\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">ThreadPoolExecutor</span>(max_workers=<span class="num">4</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> pool:\n    results = <span class="fn">list</span>(pool.map(fetch, urls))\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Threaded: {time.time()-start:.1f}s"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── CPU-Bound Example: Heavy Computation ──</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">cpu_work</span>(n):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">sum</span>(i*i <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(n))\n\nnumbers = [<span class="num">10_000_000</span>] * <span class="num">4</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Sequential</span>\nstart = time.time()\n[<span class="fn">cpu_work</span>(n) <span class="kw">for</span> n <span class="kw">in</span> numbers]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Sequential: {time.time()-start:.1f}s"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiprocessing — uses separate processes, bypasses GIL</span>\nstart = time.time()\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">ProcessPoolExecutor</span>(max_workers=<span class="num">4</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> pool:\n    results = <span class="fn">list</span>(pool.map(cpu_work, numbers))\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Multiprocessing: {time.time()-start:.1f}s"</span>)  <span class="cm"># ~4x faster</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>asyncio — modern Python concurrency</strong> for I/O-bound workloads. Instead of threads, <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> uses <em>cooperative multitasking</em>: functions voluntarily pause at <code>await</code> points, letting other tasks run. This is more efficient than threads (no context-switch overhead) and is the backbone of modern web frameworks like FastAPI:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> asyncio\n\n<span class="kw">async def</span> <span class="fn">fetch_data</span>(name: <span class="fn">str</span>, delay: <span class="fn">float</span>) -> <span class="fn">str</span>:\n    <span class="str">"""Simulate an async API call."""</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"[{name}] Starting..."</span>)\n    <span class="kw">await</span> asyncio.sleep(delay)  <span class="cm"># non-blocking wait</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"[{name}] Done after {delay}s"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">f"{name}: data loaded"</span>\n\n<span class="kw">async def</span> <span class="fn">main</span>():\n    <span class="cm"># Run 3 tasks concurrently — total time = max(delays)</span>\n    tasks = [\n        <span class="fn">fetch_data</span>(<span class="str">"API-1"</span>, <span class="num">2.0</span>),\n        <span class="fn">fetch_data</span>(<span class="str">"API-2"</span>, <span class="num">1.5</span>),\n        <span class="fn">fetch_data</span>(<span class="str">"API-3"</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>),\n    ]\n    results = <span class="kw">await</span> asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(results)  <span class="cm"># all 3 results in ~2 seconds total</span>\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: I/O vs CPU bound performance --- */
+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="cg-title">Performance: Sequential vs Threading vs Multiprocessing vs Asyncio</div></div>'
+ '<div id="plotly-concurrency" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ '(function(){'
+ 'var methods = ["Sequential", "Threading", "Multiprocessing", "Asyncio"];'
+ 'var io_times = [8.0, 2.1, 2.3, 2.0];'
+ 'var cpu_times = [12.0, 12.5, 3.2, 12.0];'
+ 'var t1 = {x:methods, y:io_times, name:"I/O-Bound Task (4 API calls)", type:"bar", marker:{color:"#4ecdc4"}};'
+ 'var t2 = {x:methods, y:cpu_times, name:"CPU-Bound Task (4 computations)", type:"bar", marker:{color:"#ff6b6b"}};'
+ 'var layout = {barmode:"group", paper_bgcolor:"transparent", plot_bgcolor:"transparent",'
+ 'font:{color:"#ccc"}, yaxis:{title:"Time (seconds)", gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.07)"},'
+ 'xaxis:{gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.07)"},'
+ 'legend:{x:0.02,y:0.98}, margin:{l:50,r:20,t:10,b:50},'
+ 'annotations:[{x:"Threading",y:2.1,text:"Best for I/O!",showarrow:true,arrowhead:2,ax:0,ay:-30,font:{color:"#4ecdc4",size:11}},'
+ '{x:"Multiprocessing",y:3.2,text:"Best for CPU!",showarrow:true,arrowhead:2,ax:0,ay:-30,font:{color:"#ff6b6b",size:11}}]};'
+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plotly-concurrency",[t1,t2],layout,{responsive:true});'
+ '})();'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1f9f5;</div><div class="cc-title">Threading</div><div class="cc-body">Best for: I/O-bound tasks. Shared memory. Limited by GIL for CPU. Use <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x2699;</div><div class="cc-title">Multiprocessing</div><div class="cc-body">Best for: CPU-bound tasks. Separate memory per process. Bypasses GIL. Use <code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x26a1;</div><div class="cc-title">Asyncio</div><div class="cc-body">Best for: I/O-bound + many connections. Single-threaded, event-loop. FastAPI, aiohttp use this.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1f4a1;</div><div class="cc-title">Rule of Thumb</div><div class="cc-body">Waiting for stuff? Threading/Asyncio. Computing stuff? Multiprocessing. Training models? Let PyTorch handle it (C++ threads bypass GIL).</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>ML context:</strong> PyTorch and NumPy release the GIL during heavy computations because their backends are written in C/C++. So <code>torch.matmul()</code> can run truly in parallel across threads. You rarely need to manage concurrency for model training — PyTorch\'s <code>DataLoader(num_workers=4)</code> handles it. But for data collection, API calls, and preprocessing pipelines, these patterns are essential.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: BEST PRACTICES & ECOSYSTEM
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Best Practices & Ecosystem</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Writing Python that works is easy. Writing Python that is maintainable, testable, and production-ready is a skill.</strong> This section covers the tools and practices that separate prototype notebooks from deployable software: virtual environments, project structure, testing, code formatting, and the packaging ecosystem. These are the habits that make you a professional Python developer.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Virtual Environments</strong> — Isolating dependencies is non-negotiable in professional Python. Every project gets its own environment:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-left"><div class="compare-title">venv (built-in)</div><div class="compare-body"><pre>python -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\npip freeze > requirements.txt</pre>Lightweight, no extra install. Standard for most projects.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-right"><div class="compare-title">conda (Anaconda)</div><div class="compare-body"><pre>conda create -n myenv python=3.12\nconda activate myenv\nconda install numpy pandas\nconda env export > env.yml</pre>Handles C/CUDA dependencies. Preferred for scientific/ML work.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Professional Project Structure</strong> — A well-organized project is easier to navigate, test, and package:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PROJECT STRUCTURE</span></div><pre class="code-block"><code>my_nlp_project/\n├── pyproject.toml        <span class="cm"># project metadata & dependencies</span>\n├── README.md\n├── .gitignore\n├── .env                  <span class="cm"># secrets (never commit!)</span>\n├── src/\n│   └── my_nlp/\n│       ├── __init__.py   <span class="cm"># makes it a package</span>\n│       ├── tokenizer.py\n│       ├── model.py\n│       ├── train.py\n│       └── utils.py\n├── tests/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── test_tokenizer.py\n│   └── test_model.py\n├── notebooks/\n│   └── exploration.ipynb\n├── data/                 <span class="cm"># .gitignore this!</span>\n│   ├── raw/\n│   └── processed/\n└── configs/\n    └── training.yaml</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>pyproject.toml</strong> — The modern standard for Python project configuration, replacing <code>setup.py</code> and <code>setup.cfg</code>:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>TOML</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># pyproject.toml — single file for all project config</span>\n[project]\nname = <span class="str">"my-nlp-project"</span>\nversion = <span class="str">"0.1.0"</span>\ndescription = <span class="str">"Sentiment analysis with transformers"</span>\nrequires-python = <span class="str">">= 3.10"</span>\ndependencies = [\n    <span class="str">"torch>=2.0"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"transformers>=4.30"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"datasets"</span>,\n]\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [<span class="str">"pytest"</span>, <span class="str">"mypy"</span>, <span class="str">"ruff"</span>, <span class="str">"black"</span>]\n\n[tool.ruff]\nline-length = <span class="num">88</span>\n\n[tool.mypy]\nstrict = <span class="kw">true</span>\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\ntestpaths = [<span class="str">"tests"</span>]</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Testing with pytest</strong> — Tests are not optional for professional code. pytest is the de facto standard: simple, powerful, and extensible:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># tests/test_tokenizer.py</span>\n<span class="kw">import</span> pytest\n<span class="kw">from</span> my_nlp.tokenizer <span class="kw">import</span> SimpleTokenizer\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_basic_tokenization</span>():\n    tok = SimpleTokenizer()\n    result = tok.tokenize(<span class="str">"Hello world"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">assert</span> result == [<span class="str">"hello"</span>, <span class="str">"world"</span>]\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_empty_input</span>():\n    tok = SimpleTokenizer()\n    <span class="kw">assert</span> tok.tokenize(<span class="str">""</span>) == []\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_special_characters</span>():\n    tok = SimpleTokenizer()\n    result = tok.tokenize(<span class="str">"don\'t stop! #NLP"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="str">"don\'t"</span> <span class="kw">in</span> result <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="str">"dont"</span> <span class="kw">in</span> result\n\n<span class="cm"># Parametrized tests — test many cases elegantly</span>\n<span class="kw">@pytest.mark.parametrize</span>(<span class="str">"text,expected_len"</span>, [\n    (<span class="str">"hello"</span>, <span class="num">1</span>),\n    (<span class="str">"hello world"</span>, <span class="num">2</span>),\n    (<span class="str">"a b c d e"</span>, <span class="num">5</span>),\n    (<span class="str">""</span>, <span class="num">0</span>),\n])\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_token_count</span>(text, expected_len):\n    tok = SimpleTokenizer()\n    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(tok.tokenize(text)) == expected_len\n\n<span class="cm"># Fixtures — shared setup for tests</span>\n<span class="kw">@pytest.fixture</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">sample_corpus</span>():\n    <span class="kw">return</span> [<span class="str">"I love NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"Python is great"</span>, <span class="str">"Deep learning rocks"</span>]\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_batch_tokenize</span>(sample_corpus):\n    tok = SimpleTokenizer()\n    results = [tok.tokenize(doc) <span class="kw">for</span> doc <span class="kw">in</span> sample_corpus]\n    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">all</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(r) > <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">for</span> r <span class="kw">in</span> results)\n\n<span class="cm"># Run: pytest -v tests/</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Code Quality Tools</strong> — Automated formatting and linting eliminate style debates and catch bugs:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">B</div><div class="cc-title">Black</div><div class="cc-body">Opinionated code formatter. Zero config. Just run <code>black .</code> and it formats everything consistently. Ends style debates forever.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">R</div><div class="cc-title">Ruff</div><div class="cc-body">Ultra-fast linter (written in Rust). Replaces flake8, isort, and more. Catches errors, enforces PEP 8, sorts imports. 10-100x faster than alternatives.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">M</div><div class="cc-title">mypy</div><div class="cc-body">Static type checker. Catches type errors before runtime. Essential for large codebases. Integrates with CI/CD.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">P</div><div class="cc-title">pre-commit</div><div class="cc-body">Git hooks that run black, ruff, mypy automatically before each commit. Install once, never commit broken code again.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Install dev tools:</strong> <code>pip install black ruff mypy pytest pre-commit</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Format code:</strong> <code>black .</code> — auto-formats all Python files to consistent style (88 char lines, double quotes, trailing commas).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Lint code:</strong> <code>ruff check . --fix</code> — finds and auto-fixes common issues: unused imports, undefined variables, PEP 8 violations.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Type check:</strong> <code>mypy src/</code> — verifies type annotations match actual usage. Start with <code>mypy --warn-return-any</code> and increase strictness gradually.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Run tests:</strong> <code>pytest -v --cov=src/</code> — run all tests with coverage report. Aim for 80%+ coverage on critical paths.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* --- Plotly: Professional Python workflow --- */
+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="cg-title">Professional Python Development Workflow</div></div>'
+ '<div id="plotly-workflow" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ '(function(){'
+ 'var steps = ["Write Code", "Format<br>(black)", "Lint<br>(ruff)", "Type Check<br>(mypy)", "Test<br>(pytest)", "Commit<br>(pre-commit)", "CI/CD<br>(GitHub Actions)"];'
+ 'var importance = [100, 85, 90, 75, 95, 80, 88];'
+ 'var adoptionPro = [100, 92, 88, 70, 90, 78, 85];'
+ 'var adoptionBeg = [100, 15, 10, 5, 20, 5, 8];'
+ 'var t1 = {x:steps, y:adoptionPro, name:"Professional Teams", type:"scatter", mode:"lines+markers",'
+ 'line:{color:"#4ecdc4",width:3}, marker:{size:10}};'
+ 'var t2 = {x:steps, y:adoptionBeg, name:"Solo Beginners", type:"scatter", mode:"lines+markers",'
+ 'line:{color:"#ff6b6b",width:3}, marker:{size:10}};'
+ 'var layout = {paper_bgcolor:"transparent", plot_bgcolor:"transparent",'
+ 'font:{color:"#ccc"}, yaxis:{title:"Adoption Rate %", gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.07)", range:[0,110]},'
+ 'xaxis:{gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.07)"},'
+ 'legend:{x:0.02,y:0.98}, margin:{l:50,r:20,t:10,b:80}};'
+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plotly-workflow",[t1,t2],layout,{responsive:true});'
+ '})();'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="ce-title">Complete Development Workflow Example</div><div class="ce-body">'
+ '<pre>$ mkdir my_nlp_project && cd my_nlp_project\n$ python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\n$ pip install torch transformers datasets\n$ pip install -e ".[dev]"          # install project + dev tools\n\n# Write code...\n$ black .                          # format\n$ ruff check . --fix               # lint + autofix\n$ mypy src/                        # type check\n$ pytest -v --cov=src/             # test + coverage\n$ git add . && git commit -m "feat: add tokenizer"  # pre-commit runs automatically\n\n# CI/CD pipeline runs same checks on GitHub\n# Deploy with: pip install . OR docker build .</pre>'
+ '</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Many data scientists skip testing, linting, and type checking because "it is just a notebook." But the moment your code moves from a notebook to a production pipeline — serving predictions, processing real data, running on a schedule — these practices become critical. The gap between a data scientist and an ML engineer is not more math. It is software engineering discipline.</div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Congratulations!</strong> You have completed the Python Core course. You now have a solid foundation spanning the entire language: from variables and memory model through data structures, functions, OOP, file handling, and now advanced topics — generators, regex, functional programming, type hints, concurrency, and professional tooling. This is the Python knowledge base that every ML and NLP engineer needs. The journey continues with NumPy, Pandas, and the ML ecosystem that builds on everything you have learned here.</div>'

